\title{TabFM Classification Learner}
\description{
Tabular foundation model developed by Google Research.
Uses \CRANpkg{reticulate} to interface with the \href{https://github.com/google-research/tabfm}{\code{tabfm}} Python package.
The model makes zero-shot predictions via in-context learning and is not trained on the data in the usual sense.
Features are passed to \code{tabfm} as a data frame, so that categorical features are ordinal encoded and missing
values are imputed by the package's own preprocessing pipeline.
}
\section{Installation}{

This learner relies on \CRANpkg{reticulate} to handle Python dependencies.
It is not necessary to install any Python package manually in advance or specify a Python environment
via \code{reticulate::use_python()}, \code{reticulate::use_virtualenv()}, \code{reticulate::use_condaenv()},
or \code{reticulate::use_miniconda()}.
By calling \verb{$train()} or \verb{$predict()}, the required Python packages will be installed automatically,
if not already.
The \code{backend} parameter selects which extra is installed:
\code{"jax"} installs \code{tabfm[jax]} and \code{"pytorch"} installs \code{tabfm[pytorch]}.
Reticulate will then configure and initialize an ephemeral environment satisfying those requirements,
unless an existing environment (e.g., \code{"r-reticulate"}) in reticulate's
\href{https://rstudio.github.io/reticulate/articles/versions.html#order-of-discovery}{Order of Discovery}
contains all the necessary packages.

You may also manually install \code{tabfm} into a Python environment following the
\href{https://github.com/google-research/tabfm#installation}{official installation guide}
and specify the environment via \verb{reticulate::use_*()} before calling \verb{$train()} or \verb{$predict()}.

On the first call to \verb{$train()}, the pre-trained model weights (about 100 MB) are downloaded from
\href{https://huggingface.co/google}{Hugging Face} and cached for subsequent calls.
}

\section{Saving a Learner}{

In order to save a \code{LearnerClassifTabFM} for later usage,
it is necessary to call the \verb{$marshal()} method on the \code{Learner}
before writing it to disk, as the object will otherwise not be saved correctly.
After loading a marshaled \code{LearnerClassifTabFM} into R again,
you then need to call \verb{$unmarshal()} to transform it into a useable state.
The several gigabyte pre-trained backbone is not serialized;
it is detached before marshaling and reloaded from the Hugging Face cache on \verb{$unmarshal()}.
}

\section{Custom mlr3 parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{backend} selects the computational backend, either \code{"jax"} or \code{"pytorch"}.
It also determines which pre-trained weights are downloaded and which Python packages are installed.
\item \code{device} is only used by the \code{"pytorch"} backend and is passed as a string to the model loader,
e.g., \code{"cpu"} or \code{"cuda"}.
The \code{"jax"} backend ignores this parameter and selects the device automatically.
\item \code{norm_methods} accepts a single string or a character vector with values from
\code{"none"}, \code{"power"}, \code{"quantile"}, \code{"quantile_rtdl"}, and \code{"robust"}.
\item \code{n_feature_crosses} and \code{n_svd_features} accept either a non-negative integer or the string \code{"sqrt"}.
\item \code{cache_context} is only supported by the \code{"pytorch"} backend.
\item \code{random_state} accepts either an integer or the special value \code{"None"}
which corresponds to \code{None} in Python.
}
}

